7,Nilo-Saharan languages | African Language Family | Britannica,https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nilo-Saharan-languages,"July 26, 1999 - Most Nilo-Saharan languages are tonal, using pitch to indicate lexical or grammatical differences, though some border on non-tonal languages. Unlike the Afro-Asiatic family, Nilo-Saharan languages generally lack gender distinctions, with only a few exceptions in some Nilotic languages.",Surbakhal,Nilo-Saharan
8,Music in Global America: MUSICAL TRADITIONS OF SUB-SAHARAN AFRICA,https://scalar.usc.edu/works/music-in-global-america/musical-traditions-of-sub-saharan-africa,Scenes show how the djembe (drum) and agogo (double bell) are made and played polyrhythmically; the melding of music and dance; transmission of music-dance culture. (10:50) Watch on YouTube so you can easily read the English subtitles. The call and response format is an exclusively vocal medium in traditional sub-Saharan African music.,Surbakhal,Nilo-Saharan
